  "content": "The user is asking about the Singleton pattern in C#. This is a well-known design pattern. I should explain what it is, why it's used, and provide a clear code example. Let me cover:\n\n1. Definition\n2. Why it's used\n3. A standard implementation (thread-safe)\n4. Brief pros/cons or considerations\n\nI'll keep it clear and practical with a code example.\n</think>\n\n# Singleton Pattern in C#\n\nA **Singleton** is a design pattern that ensures a class has **exactly one instance** and provides a global point of access to it.\n\n## Why use it?\n\n- You need a single, shared instance (e.g., a configuration manager, a logger, a database connection pool).\n- You want to prevent accidental creation of multiple instances.\n\n## Classic Implementation (Thread-Safe)\n\n```csharp\npublic sealed class Singleton\n{\n    private static readonly Singleton _instance = new Singleton();\n\n    // Private constructor prevents external instantiation\n    private Singleton() { }\n\n    // Global access point\n    public static Singleton Instance => _instance;\n\n    // Example method\n    public void DoWork()\n    {\n        Console.WriteLine(\"Singleton is doing work.\");\n    }\n}\n```\n\n### Usage\n\n```csharp\nSingleton s1 = Singleton.Instance;\nSingleton s2 = Singleton.Instance;\n\nConsole.WriteLine(ReferenceEquals(s1, s2)); // True\n```\n\n## Key Points\n\n| Aspect | Detail |\n|--------|--------|\n| **Private constructor** | Prevents `new Singleton()` from outside the class. |\n| **Static field** | Holds the single instance. |\n| **Thread-safe by default** | The static field initializer is guaranteed to run once by the CLR (lazy initialization is thread-safe). |\n| **`sealed`** | Prevents subclassing (which could bypass the pattern). |\n\n## Variations\n\n- **Lazy initialization (lock-based):** Use a `lock` if you need deferred creation with explicit control:\n\n```csharp\npublic sealed class Singleton\n{\n    private static Singleton? _instance;\n    private static readonly object _lock = new object();\n\n    private Singleton() { }\n\n    public static Singleton Instance\n    {\n        get\n        {\n            if (_instance is null)\n            {\n                lock (_lock)\n                {\n                    if (_instance is null)\n                        _instance = new Singleton();\n                }\n            }\n            return _instance;\n        }\n    }\n}\n```\n\n- **Dependency Injection alternative:** Register the class as a **singleton** in a DI container (e.g., `services.AddSingleton<ILogger>()`). This is often preferred in modern C# applications because it keeps the class testable and avoids hard-coded static access.\n\n## When to (not) use it\n\n- ✅ Good for: application-wide services, configuration, logging.\n- ⚠️ Avoid if: it introduces hidden coupling, makes unit testing harder, or you could achieve the same result with DI.",
